title: Deleting duplicate copies
tags: Duplicates
kind: guide
summary: In the duplicates panel, keep the copy under the cursor and trash the others, or trash one copy — your choice, never a heuristic's.

Which copy survives is always yours to choose. Nothing picks one for you, and no
action spans several groups. In the [[duplicates panel]], both deletions act on
the row under the cursor, and both are confirmed:

* ''keep this one'' — <<key "duplicates:keep" Shift+k>>, and the first item of the context menu: trashes every ''other'' copy of the group. The confirmation names every path it will trash, and how much space pruning the trash will then reclaim.
* ''trash this one'' — <<key "duplicates:trash" Delete>>: that copy alone, saying what the group is left with.

The copies go to the [[Trash]], so a mistake is recoverable
([[Restoring from the trash]]); the space is freed when the trash is pruned
([[Trash retention]]).

The panel updates the group at once — its count, its reclaimable space, and the
group itself, gone once a single copy is left — with the same arithmetic the
daemon applies. The daemon's own update lands a watcher quiet period later, and
the panel reloads then to confirm it, keeping the expanded groups and the cursor
where they were.

A copy deleted any other way — `mf trash -f`, a file manager — leaves its group
the same way: see [[Duplicate groups]].
